# The Recommended rule of the same name holds every other hedge.
# `relatively` sits here, because it sometimes states a real
# comparison (`relatively few nodes run the agent, compared with the
# fleet`). Most hits give no baseline (`a relatively small number of
# objects`), so the fix gives the number or the baseline. `potentially`
# stays out, because the double-hedge rule reports `could potentially`.
#
# `virtually` and `practically` each read two ways. `virtually` means
# `almost` or names a virtual copy (`virtually hoisted`), and
# `practically` means `almost` or `in practice`. The two sit here,
# because the plain sense is sometimes the one meant. `effectively`
# stays out. In 29 of its 39 hits in the measure.py corpus it meant `in
# effect` (`higher values are effectively ignored`), where dropping it
# makes the claim false, and in the other 10 it named a manner (`use
# labels effectively`).
#
# The rule hit 46 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 18 of 20 sampled
# hits softened a claim or a result. The misses stated a requirement
# (`all tests should pass regardless`) or a design (`what the pool
# should look like`). The message offers to name the condition that the
# claim depends on, which fits a writer with real doubt.
#
# `should` after a noun and before a verb that names a visible result
# (`the test should pass`, `the output should look like this`) hedges
# the result of a step, so the fix states it (`the test passes`). The
# noun must not be a pronoun, because the Recommended rule reports `it
# should work`, or a person such as `caller`, where `should` gives
# advice. `return`, `contain`, and `include` stay out, because API docs
# state a requirement with them (`implementations should return a
# copy`). The filter narrows the finding to `should`. It hit 19 lines in
# the measure.py corpus, and 15 of 19 hedged a result. The misses
# stated a requirement, a condition, or a design (`if any of those
# instances should fail`), so the branch is a suggestion.
expr person [caller, callers, consumer, consumers, user, users, reader, readers, developer, developers, operator, operators, administrator, administrators]
expr result [pass, fail, print, display, show, look, appear, succeed, output, produce, compile]
expr main [relatively, virtually, practically, <(<NOUN, !PRON, !@person> should @result), should>]
let message "Hedge. State the claim, name the condition it depends on, or drop it."
let description "Flags `relatively`, `virtually`, and `practically`, which soften a claim or read two ways, and `should` before a visible result."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
allows "Few nodes run the agent."
allows "The flag disables the cache."
allows "Higher values are effectively ignored."
allows "The test passes."
allows "The caller should pass a listener."
allows "Implementations should return a copy."
